## Key Observations & Data-Integrity Notes

**PAT divergence between accounting bases is material and explainable.** IGAAP PAT of **₹130.8 Cr** (down **38.75% YoY** from ₹213.5 Cr in FY25) contrasts sharply with IFRS PAT of **₹366.1 Cr** (up **80% YoY**). The company's own Profit Reconciliation table attributes the gap primarily to a **₹368.2 Cr positive IFRS-17 (Insurance Contracts) adjustment** in FY26, which flatters the IFRS number relative to the statutory result. Analysts should not treat these as interchangeable: the IGAAP figure is the sectoral-regulator (IRDAI) mandated number, while IFRS is explicitly labeled by the company as "not mandated by sectoral regulator."

**Underwriting quality is deteriorating on an IGAAP basis, even as IFRS metrics improve — a notable divergence.**
- IGAAP Claims Ratio rose to **68.1%** (FY26) from 61.2% (FY25)
- IGAAP Combined Ratio worsened to **103.4%** from 101.2%
- Meanwhile, the IFRS-basis combined/insurance service ratio *improved* to 101.4% from 103.0%

This split (statutory ratios deteriorating while IFRS ratios improve) is a flag worth scrutinizing further, particularly around IFRS-17 discounting and onerous-contract treatment assumptions.

**EoM ratio improved sharply** to **33.7%** (FY26) from 39.2% (FY25), aided by 1/n amortization methodology. Note: the company still has a *pending* IRDAI forbearance application relating to the FY25 EoM breach — an unresolved regulatory overhang that should be tracked in subsequent filings.

**Solvency compressed but remains comfortably above the regulatory floor.** Solvency ratio fell to **2.49x** (FY26) from 3.03x (FY25) — a ~54 bps YoY decline, still well above the 1.5x IRDAI minimum. This reflects capital consumption from premium growth (GWP **+27% YoY**) outpacing net worth accretion (**+5.3% YoY** to ₹3,219 Cr).</ans>
